half = 15
T([rows[0] + [""] + rows[0]] + [rows[1 + i] + [""] + rows[1 + half + i] for i in range(half)], [.7*cm, 1.3*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.4*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.1*cm, .3*cm, .7*cm, 1.3*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.4*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.1*cm, 1.1*cm], fs=6.5)
T([["Statistic", "Value"], ["D (max |F_n - F|)", f(q1["D"], 4)], ["Critical value, α = 0.05, n = 30 (Lilliefors 1967 table)", f(q1["crit05"], 3)], ["Approx. p-value (statsmodels, Lilliefors table method)", pf(q1["p_lilliefors"])], ["Decision", "D &gt; critical value and p &lt; 0.05 -> <b>reject H0</b>"],
   ["Cross-checks: Shapiro-Wilk / Jarque-Bera", f"W = {f(q1['shapiro_W'],3)}, p = {pf(q1['shapiro_p'])} / JB = {f(q1['jb'],1)}, p = {pf(q1['jb_p'])}"], ["Robustness: Lilliefors on log(1+π)", f"D = {f(q1['log_D'],3)}, p = {pf(q1['log_p'])} (still rejected)"], ["Lag-1 autocorrelation", f(q1["lag1_autocorr"], 2)]], [9*cm, 8*cm])
IMG("Q1.png")
P(f"<b>Interpretation.</b> Normality is <b>not reasonable</b> for Viet Nam's inflation: the empirical CDF departs from the normal CDF by D = {f(q1['D'],3)}, well above 0.161. The cause is visible: a heavy right tail (skewness {f(q1['skew'],1)}) created by the 2008 spike ({f(q1['max'],1)}%) and 2011 (18.7%), versus a calm 2015-2025 (0.6-3.6%). Log-transforming does not fix it. <b>Time-series caveat:</b> the test assumes independent observations; inflation is positively autocorrelated (lag-1 r = {f(q1['lag1_autocorr'],2)}), so the effective sample size is smaller than 30 and the stated p-value is only indicative (the table critical value is for iid data). Also, the 1996-2025 span mixes policy regimes, so 'one distribution' is itself questionable.")
P("<b>Implications.</b> (i) Do not use mean ± 1.96·s to describe 'normal' inflation ranges or to build interval forecasts for inflation; use the median/quantiles or a bootstrap. (ii) Tests on means (t, ANOVA) applied directly to inflation levels are unreliable in small samples; prefer rank-based tests or transformations and model residuals, not the raw series. (iii) For budgeting, treat inflation as skewed with tail risk (price-escalation clauses) rather than symmetric noise.")

