S.append(PageBreak()); P("Question 2 - Chi-square test of two proportions", H1)
P(f"<b>Data and outcome.</b> ILOSTAT, {q2['year']}, persons aged 15+: unemployed (UNE_TUNE_NB) and employed (EMP_TEMP_NB, ECO_SECTOR_TOTAL), Viet Nam vs Philippines (counts = ILO thousands × 1000). Binary outcome: unemployed vs employed within the labour force. <b>H0:</b> the unemployment proportion is equal in the two countries (p<sub>VN</sub> = p<sub>PH</sub>); H1: they differ.")
o = q2["observed"]; e = q2["expected"]
T([["Observed", "Unemployed", "Employed", "Total", "Unemployment rate"], ["Viet Nam", f(o[0][0], 0), f(o[0][1], 0), f(sum(o[0]), 0), f"{q2['rates']['VNM']*100:.2f}%"], ["Philippines", f(o[1][0], 0), f(o[1][1], 0), f(sum(o[1]), 0), f"{q2['rates']['PHL']*100:.2f}%"]], [3.5*cm, 3.5*cm, 3.5*cm, 3.5*cm, 3.5*cm])
T([["Expected = row total × col total / N", "Unemployed", "Employed"], ["Viet Nam", f(e[0][0], 1), f(e[0][1], 1)], ["Philippines", f(e[1][0], 1), f(e[1][1], 1)]], [6*cm, 4*cm, 4*cm])
P(f"<b>Result.</b> χ² = Σ(O-E)²/E = {f(q2['chi2'],1)}, df = (2-1)(2-1) = 1, p {pf(q2['p'])} (Yates-corrected χ² = {f(q2['chi2_yates'],1)}); all expected counts &gt; 5. Effect size: Cramér's V = {f(q2['cramers_v'],3)} (very small); difference in rates = {q2['diff']*100:.2f} pp.")
P("<b>Why this p-value must not be taken at face value.</b> These counts are <i>weighted survey estimates</i> grossed up to national populations (N ≈ 105 million), not the number of people actually interviewed. A chi-square test treats each count as an independent observation, so with ~105 million 'observations' it will flag even trivial differences, and it ignores the survey's clustering, stratification and weighting (design effect). Unweighted respondent counts were not available from ILOSTAT; the correct approach is a survey-adjusted (Rao-Scott) test on microdata. As a transparent sensitivity analysis I re-ran the test keeping the observed proportions but assuming <b>illustrative</b> effective sample sizes per country (these n are assumptions, not data):")
T([["Effective n per country (assumed)", "χ²", "p-value"]] + [[f"{s['n_eff_per_country']:,}", f(s["chi2"], 2), pf(s["p"])] for s in q2["sensitivity"]], [6*cm, 4*cm, 4*cm])
P("<b>Interpretation.</b> The <i>direction</i> (Viet Nam lower, about 0.8 pp) is stable, but whether it is statistically significant depends on the unknown effective sample size (non-significant at n<sub>eff</sub> = 2,000, significant from ~10,000). I therefore conclude that Viet Nam's measured unemployment rate is lower than the Philippines' in 2024, with a small practical effect, and that formal significance cannot be established from these aggregates.")
P("<b>Management implication.</b> Both labour markets are tight by ILO's definition, but Viet Nam's lower unemployment suggests a smaller pool of immediately available workers and more wage/retention pressure than the Philippines. If expansion needs rapid hiring of non-specialised staff, budget a recruitment premium and training pipeline in Viet Nam; the Philippines offers more slack. <b>Limitations:</b> unemployment omits informality and underemployment; one year; different survey designs.")

