P(f"<b>Independence limitation.</b> Treating successive years as independent is questionable. Within-country lag-1 autocorrelation of growth is small (VN {f(q4['lag1_autocorr']['VNM'],2)}, PH {f(q4['lag1_autocorr']['PHL'],2)}, IN {f(q4['lag1_autocorr']['IND'],2)}), but the three countries are <b>not independent groups</b>: the same years (2009, 2020) hit all of them, so the design is really repeated measures by year (a Friedman/mixed-model analysis would be more appropriate), and the 25 years are one realisation of a structurally changing economy, not random draws. Hence p-values are indicative. As the more appropriate repeated-measures check I ran a Friedman test with the 25 years as blocks: χ²(2) = {f(q4['friedman']['chi2'],2)}, p = {pf(q4['friedman']['p'])} (mean ranks within year: VN {f(q4['friedman']['mean_ranks']['VNM'],2)}, PH {f(q4['friedman']['mean_ranks']['PHL'],2)}, IN {f(q4['friedman']['mean_ranks']['IND'],2)}), which agrees with Kruskal-Wallis: the Philippines ranks lowest in a typical year.")
P("<b>Recommendation.</b> Viet Nam delivered average growth equal to India's with less than half the volatility (SD 1.35 vs 3.03 pp), which favours it for planning stability; do not claim it grows faster than India. The Philippines' growth distribution is weaker than India's.")

